{"id": "186991", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=186991", "title": "Murtosa", "text": "Murtosa is a town and municipality in Beira Litoral, Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nMurtosa (moor-TOH-zuh, /mu\u027e.\u02c8to.z\u0250/) municipality has 10,488 residents (2021 preliminary), with about 3,700 living in the town. The municipality is split in two by the Ria de Aveiro, a lagoon. The town of Torreira lies on the Atlantic Ocean, while Murtosa itself is inland on the lagoon.\nGet in.\nThe nearest train station is at Estarreja. Auto Via\u00e7\u00e3o da Murtosa] provides bus connections into town.\nThe nearest motorways are the A1 (\"Autoestrada do Norte\") from Porto and Lisbon, the A29 (\"Autoestrada da Costa da Prata\") from Vila Nova de Gaia and Albergaria-a-Velha, and the A25 (\"Autoestrada das Beiras Litoral e Alta\") from Viseu and Guarda.\nGet around.\nMurtosa has Portugal's highest rate of bicycle usage, with 17% of the population using one to get to work or school. During the summer, free bike rentals are available at three locations. There are five bike and walking paths in the municipality."}
{"id": "186993", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=186993", "title": "Astrotourism in Australia", "text": "Astrotourism is basically space tourism. While going out as an astronaut is a privilege for only a few, due to the vast clear skies in Australia, there are plenty of observatories and parks in Australia where the beauty of space can be seen.\nIf you're used to stargazing in the northern hemisphere, Australia provides a unique stargazing experience \u2013 not only because of its open skies, but as Australia is in the Southern Hemisphere, you may see some constellations the other way around, or even completely new ones. The most notable one of them all is the Southern Cross, which is resembled on the Australian flag.\nSleep.\nAs of 2025, there are two certified Dark Sky Sanctuaries offering lodging in Australia."}
{"id": "186996", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=186996", "title": "Ch\u00e2tillon-sur-Seine", "text": "Ch\u00e2tillon-sur-Seine is in the C\u00f4te-d'Or department in Bourgogne-Franche-Comt\u00e9. Ch\u00e2tillon has the label \"ville fleurie\" (city of flowers) at the \"two flowers\" level (out of four). It has also been named one of the \"most beautiful detours in France\". In addition to the advantages of the city, the churches of the 12th century, the charm of nearby villages as \"la route du cr\u00e9mant\" winds through the vineyards of the Ch\u00e2tillonnais North with two local museums vines and wine in Chaumont-le-Bois and Massingy. \nUnderstand.\nCh\u00e2tillon is in the heart of a region rich in archeology (Celtic civilization of Mont Lassois, Gallo-Roman period of Vertillum, vestiges of numerous abbeys and castles).\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe city is at the heart of a major road junction and benefits from its proximity to the A5 and A6 motorways. It is:\nBy train.\nMobigo provides shuttles from train stations in Dijon, Montbard and Troyes. You can arrive from Paris in 1 hour 50 minutes by the Montbard TGV station and the Ch\u00e2tillon-sur-Seine - Montbard bus. \nBy plane.\nThe closest airports are those of Auxerre-Branches at 78 km and Dijon Bourgogne-Longvic at 79 km. The Ch\u00e2tillon-sur-Seine aerodrome is on the edge of the city of Ch\u00e2tillon and is used by the A\u00e9roclub du Ch\u00e2tillonnais.\nSee.\nThe churches of Saint-Vorles, Saint-Nicolas and Saint-Pierre are classified as historical monuments, as are the building of the current municipal library, the Philandrier house, the menhir of Ch\u00e2tillon - in the park of the town hall - and the ruins of the castle the Dukes of Burgundy (12th century), in which is a cemetery. Sixteen other monuments are listed in the inventory.\nAmong the most emblematic places are:\nRemains of walls unified and strengthened in the 17th century still exist in some places:\nAlso to see:\nEat.\nSixteen restaurants are listed on the territory of the municipality.\nSleep.\nFour hotels with a total capacity of around 70 rooms are listed on the territory of the municipality."}
{"id": "187005", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187005", "title": "Tiszaf\u00fcred", "text": "Tiszaf\u00fcred is a town in Lake Tisza region in the east of Hungary near the Hortob\u00e1gy National Park. It is a favourite tourist spot and offers many recreational activities such as water skiing, bush walking, and thermal baths.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nDirect trains from Budapest-Keleti p\u00e1lyaudvar are available once a day at summertime. Other times you have to transfer in F\u00fczesabony. The whole journey from Budapest takes more than 2 hours and costs \u2013. Other direct connections are:\nBy bus.\nBuses are available three times a day from Budapest-Stadion. The journey takes about 3\u00bd hours and costs \u2013.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nVol\u00e1nbusz operates local buses in Tiszaf\u00fcred. Tickets (in 2021):. Passes (in 2021): per month. Tickets can be purchased at the driver. Public transport map of Tiszaf\u00fcred.\nSee.\nIn Tiszaf\u00fcred:\nIn the surroundings:\nConnect.\nTiszaf\u00fcred's postal code is H-5350, and its telephone area code is 59."}
{"id": "187006", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187006", "title": "Marksville", "text": "Marksville is the county seat of Avoyelles Parish in the River Parishes region.\nUnderstand.\nMarksville, in the heart of former slave-heavy cotton growing country, is notably the place where Solomon Northup made his way to freedom with the help of the townspeople and Northerners after spending 12 years in bondage on Avoyelles Parish plantations, as chronicled in the book and film \"12 Years A Slave\".\nGet in.\nMarksville is one of those places where you are kind of on your own getting there, as intercity public transport is not much of a reality. The main access way is State Hwy 1 that connects to Alexandria and New Roads.\nSleep.\nThe property with the largest array of amenities is likely the Paragon Casino Resort. Also, there's the:"}
{"id": "187007", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187007", "title": "Pombal", "text": "Pombal is a city and municipality in Beira Litoral, Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nPombal (pohm-BAHL, /p\u00f5.\u02c8ba\u026b/) municipality has 51,178 residents (2021 preliminary), with about 11,000 living in the city."}
{"id": "187011", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187011", "title": "Santa Comba D\u00e3o", "text": "Santa Comba D\u00e3o is a city and municipality in Beira Alta, Portugal at the confluence of the D\u00e3o and Mondego Rivers.\nUnderstand.\nSanta Comba D\u00e3o (SUHN-tuh KOHM-buh DUHNW, /\u02c8s\u0250\u0303.t\u0250 \u02c8k\u00f5.b\u0250 \u02c8d\u0250\u0303w/) municipality has 10,642 residents (2021 preliminary), with about 3,300 living in the city."}
{"id": "187016", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187016", "title": "Penafiel", "text": "Penafiel is a city and municipality in Portugal's Douro Litoral region. The municipality is home to several medieval churches that are national monuments.\nUnderstand.\nPenafiel (p(ih)-nuh-fee-EHL, /p\u0268.n\u0250.fi.\u02c8\u025b\u026b/) municipality has 69,687 residents (2021 preliminary), with about 18,950 living in the city."}
{"id": "187024", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187024", "title": "Southwestern Iran", "text": "Southwestern Iran is a region of Western Iran.\nRegions.\nSouthwestern Iran includes 3 of 30 provinces in Iran:\nGet in.\nBy train.\nThere is only one mainline in this part of Iran, running in a north-south direction. The line connects Khorramshahr in the south with Qom via Ahwaz and Dezful and onto Tehran. There are several trains per day, including overnight options. An international railway link with Basra, in neighboring Iraq, has been under construction for a long time. It \"might\" open for traffic around 2025."}
{"id": "187034", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187034", "title": "Central Western Iran", "text": "Central Western Iran is in Western Iran. It is made up of 6 provinces:\nTalk.\nPersian, Kurdish and Azeri."}
{"id": "187051", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187051", "title": "F\u00fczesabony", "text": "F\u00fczesabony is a town of 7,200 people (2023) in Lake Tisza region on the northern edge of the Great Plain. It is transport hub for road and rail. Its railway station was designed by the renowned architect Ferenc Pfaff.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nDirect trains from Budapest-Keleti p\u00e1lyaudvar via G\u00f6d\u00f6ll\u0151 and Hatvan are available in 30\u201360 minutes. The whole journey from Budapest takes 1\u00bd hours and costs \u2013. Other direct connections are:\nGet around.\nThere is no local public transport or taxi service. You can use the regional buses operated by Vol\u00e1nbusz.\nConnect.\nF\u00fczesabony's postal code is H-3390, and its telephone area code is 36."}
{"id": "187054", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187054", "title": "Tandzaver", "text": "Tandzaver is a small village in Southern Armenia, famous as the first land-art museum in Armenia. There is a beautiful forest where the community built land art pieces that are collectively referred to as Magic Forest.\nUnderstand.\nTandzaver is located on a road that before 2021 was not in a good shape and the village enjoyed few visitors. However as of August 2021 the road is undergoing a major renovation and getting wider to become the main road from Goris to Kapan.\nThe nearest mountain to Tandzaver is Mount Ashkharates (2063 meters), which is kind of a symbol for the locals. \nGet in.\nYou can take a taxi from the village of Tatev. There are also numerous trails you can hike to the village by.\nGet around.\nYou can walk from one edge of the village to the other in 20 minutes.\nDo.\nHike, bike and horseback ride.\nBuy.\nThere is one small shop in the village selling necessities.\nEat.\nYou can buy some basic food from a small village shop or eat at local B&amp;Bs.\nDrink.\nThere are local homemade fruit vodkas."}
{"id": "187060", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187060", "title": "Nisa (Portugal)", "text": "Nisa is a town and municipality in Alto Alentejo, Portugal.\nUnderstand.\nNisa (NEE-zuh, /\u02c8ni.z\u0250/) municipality has 5,951 residents (2021 preliminary).\nGet in.\nRede Expressos[dead link] offers long-distance intercity express bus service.\nRodovi\u00e1ria do Alentejo provides regional bus service.\nThe nearest motorway is the A23 (\"Autoestrada da Beira Interior\") from Guarda, Covilh\u00e3, Castelo Branco, Abrantes, and Torres Novas."}
{"id": "187075", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=187075", "title": "London on foot", "text": "London is one of the greatest cities in the world, as there's something for everyone to see and do. Below is a selection of walks of varying lengths and difficulty that cover the vast majority of the city as well as its suburbs and some of the home counties.\nFor interesting walks in central London with lots of famous sights along the way, try the London Wall walk and the South Bank Walk. Transport for London (TfL) also have a selection of walks on their website, which like these walks, are of varying lengths and difficulty.\nSome considerations.\nGeneral notes.\nif you're doing a walk in multiple chunks.\nAccommodation.\nSee relevant London district articles for listings.\nShort walks (Under 5 miles).\nThe easiest walks that are highly wheelchair/disability and pet friendly.\nPaddington station to Camden Market.\nWalk length: 2.7 mi\nFor a disability/wheelchair friendly version of this walk, you can catch a boat run by the London Waterbus Company (Little Venice), which runs a service from Little Venice to Camden Market along most of the walking route.\nCutty Sark station to the London Cable Car (South Terminal).\nWalk length: 2.15 mi\nKing\u2019s Cross station to Paddington station.\nWalk length: 4.7 mi\nSouthwark Cathedral to Westminster Cathedral.\nWalk length: 3.6 mi\nLondon Cable Car (North Terminal) to Royal Victoria station loop.\nWalk length: 1.2 mi\nHammersmith Tube station to Edgware Road Tube station.\nWalk length: 3.4 mi\nWaterloo station to Bank Tube station.\nWalk length: 1.5 mi\nMedium walks (5 to 10 miles).\nSlightly longer walks that can easily be done in a morning or afternoon.\nTower Hill Tube station to Paddington station.\nWalk length: 8.4 mi\nThe Woolwich Ferry to the Golden Hinde.\nWalk length: 8.7 mi\nKenwood House to Westminster Abbey.\nWalk length: 6.7 mi\nLondon Bridge station to Langham Place.\nWalk length: 7.4 mi\